Subject: [PATCH] input: retroid: suspend and resume the gamepad MCU across system sleep

Same class of bug as rsinput's own suspend/resume fix (AYN devices,
confirmed via shuuri-labs/pocknix-os's own
1004-input-rsinput-suspend-resume-gamepad-mcu.patch): the MCU streams
input reports continuously over the UART. Across a system-suspend
teardown it keeps sending, so the geni-serial RX interrupt storms while
the port is stopping, the primary handler returns IRQ_NONE, and the
kernel's spurious-IRQ detector permanently disables the UART IRQ - the
gamepad is then dead after resume until reboot. This driver (0008) had no
suspend/resume handling at all before this patch.

Add PM ops: on suspend, quiesce the MCU (drop its enable rail and assert
reset) so the line goes silent before the UART suspends; on resume,
re-run the exact same power-up sequence and init handshake probe() uses
(gpio dance + gamepad_send_init_sequence) rather than assuming a lighter
resume path is enough - this MCU's init sequence is a multi-step
handshake (see gamepad_send_init_sequence's own six-step config write),
not just a power rail toggle.

UNVERIFIED on real hardware as of this commit - the mechanism is
well-founded (same bug class as a real, credited upstream fix for the
sibling rsinput driver) but this needs an actual suspend/resume cycle on
a real device to confirm the gamepad survives it, which wasn't available
when this was written.
---
diff -rupbN linux.orig/drivers/input/joystick/retroid.c linux/drivers/input/joystick/retroid.c
--- linux.orig/drivers/input/joystick/retroid.c
+++ linux/drivers/input/joystick/retroid.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
  */

 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
@@ -461,6 +462,55 @@
 	return 0;
 }

+static int gamepad_mcu_uart_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct gamepad_device *gamepad_dev = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * The MCU streams gamepad reports continuously over the UART. If it
+	 * keeps sending across the system-suspend teardown, the geni-serial
+	 * RX interrupt storms while the port is being stopped, the primary
+	 * handler returns IRQ_NONE, and the kernel's spurious-IRQ detector
+	 * permanently disables the UART IRQ - leaving the gamepad dead after
+	 * resume until the IRQ is re-requested (i.e. reboot). Quiesce the MCU
+	 * (drop its enable rail, hold it in reset) so the line goes silent
+	 * before the UART suspends; resume() re-powers and re-initialises it.
+	 */
+	if (gamepad_dev->enable_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(gamepad_dev->enable_gpio, 0);
+
+	if (gamepad_dev->reset_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(gamepad_dev->reset_gpio, 0);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int gamepad_mcu_uart_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct gamepad_device *gamepad_dev = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	struct serdev_device *serdev = to_serdev_device(dev);
+
+	if (gamepad_dev->reset_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(gamepad_dev->reset_gpio, 0);
+
+	msleep(100);
+
+	if (gamepad_dev->enable_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(gamepad_dev->enable_gpio, 1);
+
+	if (gamepad_dev->reset_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(gamepad_dev->reset_gpio, 1);
+
+	msleep(100);
+
+	gamepad_send_init_sequence(serdev);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static DEFINE_SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(gamepad_mcu_uart_pm_ops,
+				gamepad_mcu_uart_suspend, gamepad_mcu_uart_resume);
+
 static const struct of_device_id gamepad_mcu_uart_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "retroid,retroid-pocket-gamepad" },
 	{}
@@ -470,6 +520,7 @@
 static struct serdev_device_driver gamepad_mcu_uart_driver = {
 	.driver = {
 		.name = DRIVER_NAME,
+		.pm = pm_sleep_ptr(&gamepad_mcu_uart_pm_ops),
 		.of_match_table = gamepad_mcu_uart_of_match,
 	},
 	.probe = gamepad_mcu_uart_probe,
